		{
			"id": 2030727,
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://front-end.social/@AmeliaBR/117242034574188743",
			"author": {
				"name": "Amelia Bellamy-Royds",
				"url": "https://front-end.social/@AmeliaBR",
				"avatarUrl": "https://avatars.webmention.io/cdn.masto.host/28c3647c2db4806feca362be35e12a8cb93dfeac759fe1785dc6e65927e94463.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2026-09-09T16:31:14.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> So, to be clear: you are still building 474 open graph *images*, you're just not building 474 separate static HTML pages just to screenshot them to make the images. </p><p>You're using the Chromium-based screenshot service as the build tool instead of using eleventy plus also the screenshot service.</p><p>That seems like a sensible use of resources. But its not quite as impressive as you implied yesterday; your total build time including the screenshots is still proportional to page count.</p>",
			"type": "reply",
			"via": "webmention"
		},
		{
